The sources describe **tranquility (samatha)** and **insight (vipassanā)** as two qualities that **share in clear knowing**. Their development is central to the training path in the holy life, leading to profound benefits and ultimately, release from suffering and stress.

Here is a high-level PlantUML activity diagram illustrating where these qualities fit within the training context:

```plantuml
@startuml
title Workflow of Tranquility & Insight in the Training Path

start

:Starting Point: **Uninstructed Run-of-the-Mill Person**;

group "Foundational Practices for a Noble Disciple"
  :Continuously **Cultivate Virtue**\n(Restraint, good behavior, guard sense faculties);
  :Continuously **Develop Heedfulness**\n(Mindfulness, Alertness, Persistence);
  note right
    These form the ethical and attentional basis for deeper practice.
  end note
end group

group "The Gradual Training Path"
  :Engage with Dhamma\n(Listen & Attend Appropriately);
  :Sensitivity to Meaning & Dhamma arises;
  note right
    This sensitivity leads to a sequence of mental states:
    Joy -> Rapture -> Calm -> Pleasure -> Mind becoming **Concentrated**
  end note

  split
    partition "Development of Tranquility (Samatha)"
      #lightgreen:Purpose: **Develop the Mind** & **Abandon Passion**;
      :Practice Seclusion\n(from sensuality & unskillful qualities);
      :Enter & Remain in Jhānas (States of Deep Concentration);
      :Mind becomes **Pliant, Malleable, Luminous**\n(Result of Heightened Mind Training);
    end partition

    partition "Development of Insight (Vipassanā)"
      #lightblue:Purpose: **Develop Discernment** & **Abandon Ignorance**;
      :Discern the **Four Noble Truths**\n(Stress, Origination, Cessation, Path);
      :See Phenomena "**As They Have Come To Be**"\n(e.g., inconstant, stressful, not-self);
      :Leads to **Piercing the Mass of Ignorance**;
    end partition
  split again

  note left of "Development of Tranquility (Samatha)"
    **Tranquility** refers to the development of stillness and calm, often through concentration practices like the jhānas. This leads to a developed mind and the abandonment of passion. The mind becomes soft, pliant, and luminous.
  end note

  note right of "Development of Insight (Vipassanā)"
    **Insight** refers to the development of clear seeing and understanding, particularly through discerning the Four Noble Truths and the impermanent, stressful, and not-self nature of phenomena. This leads to developed discernment and the abandonment of ignorance.
  end note

  note bottom
    **Tranquility and Insight** are said to **occur in tandem** and are both crucial for reaching higher states of knowledge and release, even aiding in the cessation of perception and feeling. The path is a **gradual training, gradual action, gradual practice**.
  end note

  :**Abandon Passion, Aversion, & Delusion**;
  :Attain **Awareness-Release & Discernment-Release**;
  :Realize **Ending of Suffering & Stress (Unbinding)**;
end group

end
@enduml
```